    "prose": "Rain rattled harder above the workshop, and wind swept through the wide gate. Mert still stood between Aya and the approach. The restored mosaic panel remained intact where they had left it.\n\n“Take your side,” Mert said. “We lift on three, then stop and check our hold.”\n\nAya almost answered that she was ready without checking. Instead, she placed herself opposite him and waited.\n\n“One,” Mert said.\n\nAya watched his stance and matched it.\n\n“Two.”\n\nThey each set both hands carefully against their own side of the panel.\n\n“Three.”\n\nAya and Mert lifted the mosaic panel together. Its full weight came into their shared hold, steady between them. Neither tried to take more than the other.\n\n“Hold there,” Mert said.\n\nAya resisted the urge to move ahead. She adjusted only enough to keep her side level, following his pace. The panel stayed intact.\n\n“Mine is steady,” she said.\n\n“So is mine.”\n\nFor a moment they remained still, carrying the panel together while rain drummed beyond the workshop. Aya looked toward the gate, but she did not rush for it.\n\nMert shifted his body away from the gate approach. The movement visibly opened the safety boundary that he had been maintaining.\n\nAya saw him release the yard gate approach, and she accepted that the way was now clear. She kept her feet planted inside the workshop and maintained her side of the shared hold.\n\n“Good,” she said. “I’ll wait for your count before we move.”\n\nMert gave a brief nod. “No sudden turns.”\n\nAya looked across the intact mosaic at him. “No rushing.”\n\nThey remained in the workshop with the panel securely carried between them, the gate boundary released and the covered route waiting beyond.",
